python3 scripts/db.py resolve-candidate --candidate-id 124 --disposition ADMITTED --admitted-ref-id REF-01007 --session "$S" \
 --redescription "ADMITTED AS REF-01007, T3 (clinical, lower_control), AND TWO THINGS THE STAGED ROW SAID ARE CORRECTED FROM THE SOURCE. Read in full from the Europe PMC JATS XML (PMC7999795, persisted as retrieval-log/<session>/2c65a16afca1967b.xml); bibliographic fields from the Crossref payload. (1) THE TIER GUESS WAS WRONG. The row guessed tier 2 because 'a national facility audit is closer to systematic evidence than to grey primary'. It is neither: it is a SECONDARY ANALYSIS of a cross-sectional census (PMAQ-AB first cycle, 2012) of 38,812 public primary health care facilities, which the ladder places at T3 clinical lower_control. Scale does not make a census a synthesis. (2) THE HOPE THAT IT 'TABULATES MEASURED GRADIENTS' IS FALSE, AND THE SOURCE SAYS WHY. The instrument is 22 yes/no items; the ramp item is 'Does the health facility have access ramp?' -- presence only, no gradient. The authors give the reason in their introduction: tools that require 'measurement of slopes of ramps and widths of doors' are complex, 'As a consequence, few large-scale accessibility audits have been conducted'. So the one national audit in this cluster bought its scale by not measuring gradient. Filed on parameter 3 as claim_type ABSENT, which is the honest reading and is itself a finding about the audit literature. WHAT STANDS FROM THE STAGED ROW: harm_finding=1 -- access ramp present at 44 percent of facilities, handrail at the entrance 8 percent, accessible entrance 35 percent, all worse in the poorer North/Northeast and in small municipalities; the authors call it a violation of the right to health under the UNCRPD and Brazilian law. R13 graded PROXY: nobody disabled took part in the measurement in any role. NOT Co-1 on any reading. Backward pass run on admission (exec 95); it staged candidates 129 and 130." 2>&1 | tail -4
